{:edb/db-before-t 975 :edb/db-after-t 976 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534288 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:37:16.289Z" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186124521 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417703 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186124631 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417704 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186125164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123584 13194139534288 17592186045417 false] [17592186125164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417700 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186125164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186125164 1071 "Let $T: R^{2} → R^{2}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. Consider the following statement:\n\n> *If $[1,-1]^{T} ∉ Im(T)$, then $__________$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $Ker(T) = {0}$\n2. $rank(T) \\ne 2$\n3. $T$ is invertible" 13194139534288 17592186045417 false] [17592186125164 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1,-1]^{T}\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$\n- $T$ is invertible" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186125164 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{2}$ into ${R}^{2},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=2 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not lie in $\\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417684 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417705 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417684 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417706 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417684 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417707 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417684 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417684 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417684 1046 #edb/ref 17592186417707 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417684 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417684 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049371 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417684 1053 #uuid "a321f5ff-d1ce-5778-90d0-3e0d34ad246d" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417685 1080 "I, II, and III" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417685 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417685 1083 #uuid "2f10238c-7d05-58b9-96e8-9c69a8801161" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417686 1080 "II and III only" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417686 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417686 1083 #uuid "64373e5d-5dd7-5b8c-997e-b1a1d51821a3" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417687 1080 "II and III only" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417687 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417687 1083 #uuid "6bdc2284-27e4-5704-8274-3bbae725fe40" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417688 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534288 17592186045417 true] [17592186417688 1082 #ed